The new script is event_log_analyzer.py. You run it the same way as before: set INPUT_PATH, pass the file path, or use the file picker. I tested it on a 760,000-line file and it finished in about 4 seconds.

A note on “all” event IDs: Windows has thousands of event IDs, spread across hundreds of different logging sources, and many sources reuse the same numbers. So a single complete list isn’t possible. Here’s what the script covers:

The Windows Security log, in full. This is every audit event Microsoft documents for Vista / Server 2008 and later, roughly 400 IDs. It covers logons, account changes, Kerberos, object access, policy changes, the firewall, certificate services, and more. Your DC01.AFC.com data comes from this log.
Common events from other logs. These include System log startup, shutdown, and crash events; services being installed or crashing; Remote Desktop sessions; PowerShell; Task Scheduler; Windows Defender; and Sysmon.
Some IDs show up in more than one log with different meanings. For example, 7001 means different things for Service Control Manager and for Winlogon. The script looks at which program logged the event to pick the right description.

If an event isn’t in the reference, it’s labeled “Not in reference table,” and the summary lists those events so nothing gets hidden.

The other information it decodes:

Logon types 0–13. Each gets a name and a plain-English meaning.
Success or failure. Every event is marked Audit Success or Audit Failure, taken from the event’s own success/failure flag.
Failure codes. For example, 0xC000006A means the password was wrong, and 0xC0000234 means the account is locked out.
Kerberos result codes, encryption types, and pre-authentication types. For example, 0x18 on a Kerberos failure means a bad password.
Coded values. Some fields contain codes like %%2313 instead of text; these are translated (that one means “Unknown user name or bad password”).
Well-known accounts. Built-in account IDs like SYSTEM or ANONYMOUS LOGON are labeled.
Other fields. Source IP and port, workstation, process, and authentication package each get their own column. Any remaining fields are decoded into a “Details” column.
The summary also shows:

Failed logons broken down by account, by failure reason, and by source IP.
A breakdown of logon types.
Quick lookup without a file: you can check a single code directly, for example:

python event_log_analyzer.py --lookup 4740
python event_log_analyzer.py --lookup 0xC000006A
The full results are saved to a _clean.csv file and a _report.txt file next to your input, as before. The CSV now includes a marker that makes Excel display special characters correctly.


