Also add a section that flags any suspicious logs such as timestamps occurring in the early hours of the morning or constant log ons/offs or anything else that an security analyst would find worrying

The report now has a Suspicious activity tab between Summary and Events. It lists each finding with a severity (High, Medium or Low), the account or source involved, the time range, the number of events, and a plain-English reason it matters. Click any finding to see exactly the events behind it.

What it looks for

High severity:

Successful logon after repeated failures, which can mean a password was guessed.
Password spraying, where one IP address fails against many different accounts.
Audit log cleared, a classic way to cover tracks.
Changes to privileged groups, such as someone being added to Domain Admins.
Defender disabled or malware detected.
Medium severity:

Password guessing: 5 or more failed logons on one account within 10 minutes.
Off-hours activity by real users. Remote Desktop or keyboard logons rank higher than background network logons.
Rapid repeated logons/logoffs: 60 or more within 10 minutes by one person.
Logon attempts on disabled, expired or locked accounts, or with user names that don’t exist.
Account lockouts.
New user accounts and password resets done by someone else.
New services or scheduled tasks, which is how malware often survives reboots.
Audit policy or firewall changes.
Kerberos attack signs, such as Kerberoasting and AS-REP roasting.
Low severity: logons from many sources in one hour, use of the built-in Administrator account, pass-the-hash-style logons, and clock changes made by users.

Computer accounts (names ending in $) and system accounts are left out of the off-hours and logon/logoff checks. They log on around the clock normally and would bury the real findings.

Other changes

The Events tab has a new Flags column showing which rules each event triggered.
There’s a new “Flagged events only” checkbox on the Events tab.
The Summary tab shows two new totals: high-severity findings and other findings.
The terminal prints the top 15 findings when the script runs.
One setting you should check: event times are in UTC, so “off-hours” depends on UTC_OFFSET_HOURS (around line 44). It’s set to 0 right now. If the AFC computers were on US Eastern time, set it to -4 (summer) or -5 (winter); otherwise 3 AM UTC is actually 11 PM local. The off-hours window is set to midnight to 6 AM by default, and every other threshold sits right below that setting if a rule turns out too noisy or too quiet for your log.

I tested it on a log with each of these patterns planted in it. All of them were caught, and the normal daytime traffic wasn’t flagged. A 95,000-event file finished in about 9 seconds.

Treat the findings as leads to investigate, not confirmed attacks. For example, a backup job running under someone’s account could look like rapid logons.


